<h2><strong>Electric Car Insurance Requirements in Florida</strong></h2>
<p>In Florida, drivers of electric vehicles are required to follow the same steps to obtain car insurance as those with conventional cars. Electric car policies typically include coverage for Bodily Injury, Personal Injury Protection (PIP), Uninsured Motorist, and Comprehensive coverage.</p>
<p>Before you register a vehicle with at least four wheels in Florida, you must show proof of Personal Injury Protection (PIP) and Property Damage Liability (PDL). PIP covers 80% of all necessary and reasonable medical expenses up to $10,000, no matter who caused the crash. PDL covers $10,000 in damage.</p>
<p>However, it’s important to note that while electric car insurance requirements are similar to those of traditional vehicles, the cost can be different. In Florida, coverage costs for electric cars can average $313 per month but can get as high as $782 per month.</p>
<h3>What About Golf Carts?</h3>
<p>While golf cart insurance isn’t required in Florida, the state does require liability insurance for street-legal golf carts and other low-speed vehicles. Also, federal and state laws increasingly treat neighborhood electric vehicles differently, and insurance for NEVs often falls short of registration and insurance requirements.</p>
<p>Lastly, any EV charging station installed must be done so by a licensed electrical contractor and meet all state and local codes.</p>
